# Workspace Guardrails
- Do not modify any code under `HPCPerfStats/monitor/` (including the path form `@HPCPerfStats/monitor`). Inspection and analysis are allowed.
- If the user explicitly requests changes inside that directory, ask for confirmation and pause until they explicitly allow proceeding.

## Monitor Executable -> RabbitMQ -> listend.py Link
- You may inspect and analyze how the executable built from `@HPCPerfStats/monitor/` publishes output to RabbitMQ and how those messages are consumed/handled by `@HPCPerfStats/hpcperfstats/listend.py`.
- Do not treat this message-flow inspection as justification to modify `@HPCPerfStats/monitor/` sources.

## External API Client Convention
- Treat `@hpcperfstats-tools` as the client for the API exposed by the Django site.
- Keep API contract concerns (endpoints, auth headers, request/response shapes, retries/timeouts) inside `@hpcperfstats-tools`.
- Do not duplicate business rules in `@hpcperfstats-tools`; instead, rely on server-side Django/DRF logic and validate responses against expected serializers/schemas.
- When changing an API endpoint in Django, update `@hpcperfstats-tools` accordingly and add/adjust tests that cover the client-server contract.

## Service configurations
- web (gunicorn) has an ssl-enabled proxy using nginx. This proxy is defined in HPCPerfStats/services-conf/nginx-withssl.conf
- db (postgres) and redis (redis) are configured using the "command" directive in docker-compose.yaml
- all name resolution comes from the docker-compose files.

## System Configurations
- All services defined in the HPCPerfStats git folder are started in docker-compose.yaml
- All configurations for services are in `HPCPerfStats/services-conf/`
- Use Linux/System Administration best practices for security and configuration whenever modifying any system configurations.

## Example files
 - `*.example` files are templates meant to be copied/renamed by the user for a real deployment.
 - When reviewing config, use the `.example` as the intended shape/defaults, but **do not assume** the non-`.example` file exists in the workspace.
 - Avoid editing `*.example` unless you are intentionally changing the recommended defaults/documentation.
 - See `README.md` for instructions on how to copy/rename and edit these files for a production setup.